<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cleisthenes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 570 – after 508 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcmaeonidae">Alcmaeonidae</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cleisthenes was an Athenian statesman of the Alcmaeonid family, regarded as the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athenian-democracy">Athenian democracy</a>. After the fall of the tyrant Hippias, he reorganised the citizen body in 508–507 BCE into ten new tribes based on local demes, broke the power of the old aristocratic clans and created the Council of Five Hundred. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostracism">Ostracism</a> is also traditionally credited to him.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Historians estimate that Cleisthenes was born around 570 BCE. He belonged to the aristocratic Alcmaeonid clan and was the younger son of Megacles and Agariste, which made him the maternal grandson of the tyrant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleisthenes-of-sicyon">Cleisthenes of Sicyon</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peisistratus">Pisistratus</a> took power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> as a tyrant, he exiled his opponents and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcmaeonidae">Alcmaeonidae</a>. After Pisistratus died in 527 BCE, Cleisthenes returned and became the eponymous archon, but Pisistratus's successors Hipparchus and Hippias exiled him again. He then turned to the Oracle of Delphi, which accepted his plea for help against the tyranny because his family had earlier helped rebuild the sanctuary after a fire.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>With Spartan help and the support of his clan, Cleisthenes took part in overthrowing Hippias in 510 BCE. He then rivalled Isagoras, who gained the upper hand by appealing to the Spartan king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleomenes-i">Cleomenes I</a>, and Cleisthenes went into exile. Isagoras tried to dissolve the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boule-ancient-greece">Boule</a>, the council of citizens that ran the city's daily affairs. The council resisted and the people backed it. Isagoras and his partisans were besieged on the Acropolis and left the city on the third day. Cleisthenes and the exiles were recalled, and he took the lead in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>.</p><p>He replaced the four traditional tribes, based on family ties, with ten tribes drawn from residence in demes. The demes, perhaps 139 in number though this is debated, were grouped in thirds called trittyes, one each from the city, coastal and inland regions. He had citizens named by their deme rather than by their father's name, and he reorganised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boule-ancient-greece">Boule</a> from 400 members to 500, fifty from each tribe. Appointment to office by lot is also credited to him. Cleisthenes called his reforms isonomia, equality before the law, rather than demokratia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Cleisthenes may also have introduced ostracism, first used in 487 BCE, by which a vote of at least 6,000 citizens could exile a man for ten years. In 507 BCE, probably at his instigation, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> sent envoys to Artaphernes, the Persian satrap at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>, seeking help against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, the envoys agreed to give earth and water as a sign of submission and were censured on their return. No ancient text mentions Cleisthenes after his reforms, and his later life is unknown. Historians call him the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athenian-democracy">Athenian democracy</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cleisthenes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Cleisthenes</b>' reforms of 508/507 BCE, after the fall of the tyrant Hippias, created the democracy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After the tyrant Hippias was expelled in 510 BCE, the aristocrats Isagoras and <b>Cleisthenes</b> contended for power.</blockquote><a href="/government/direct-democracy">Direct democracy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>After Harmodius and Aristogeiton killed Hipparchus in 514 BCE, Hippias grew harsher, and in 510 BCE a Spartan army drove him out; the reforms of <b>Cleisthenes</b> followed in 508/507 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/government/tyranny">Tyranny · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>The first well-documented direct democracy was that of Athens, from the reforms of <b>Cleisthenes</b> in 508–507 BCE until 322 BCE, when the Macedonian regent Antipater limited citizenship to men owning at least 2,000 drachmas.</blockquote><a href="/essays/who-should-rule">Who Should Rule?</a></li><li><blockquote>From the reforms of <b>Cleisthenes</b> in 508 BCE the Assembly of all adult male citizens governed the city, so its ladder begins not with a ruler but with the richest citizens.</blockquote><a href="/societies/athens">Social Hierarchy of Classical Athens</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Classical Athens, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Classical Athens, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.97,23.72&amp;year=-530" aria-label="Classical Athens on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5141 799 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Cleisthenes"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5591 1069h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5591 1069h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.97,23.72&amp;year=-530">What was here in 530 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
